WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.692
"The Benefits of Gum Chewing
for Halitosis (Bad Breath)"

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.260
What is the impact of chewing
gum on bad breath?

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:15.720
In the Journal of Evidence-Based
Dental Practice,

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:18.480
a study compared the levels
of stinky breath compounds

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:22.980
in people’s mouths before and after
chewing mint gum for 15 minutes,

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:26.220
and they found that chewing gum
significantly decreased

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:29.220
the levels of all the volatile
sulfur compounds they tested,

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:33.000
reducing them all by about half,
which they attribute to the effect

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.497
that chewing gum
has on salivation.

00:00:35.497 --> 00:00:38.220
How do we know the mint didn’t
have anything to do with it?

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:41.700
Because flavorless chewing gum
base without any active ingredient

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:45.420
can work too; so, chewing gum
may indeed control bad breath

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:48.723
temporarily because it triggers
the salivary flow.

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:51.480
That’s thought to be why we wake
up with morning breath,

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:53.760
but it just goes
away on its own

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:55.620
even if you don’t brush
your teeth or anything.

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:58.680
Here’s the levels of the stinky
volatile sulfur compounds

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:00.480
in people’s breath right
when they wake up,

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:03.360
dropping even before
they eat breakfast.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:07.680
That’s because we have 1 1/2 liters
—that’s like 6 cups—

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.380
of saliva bathing
down our tongue daily,

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:13.020
but the spigot
is turned down at night

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:16.020
and bacteria grow
in the stagnant puddle

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:18.207
that is your nighttime mouth.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:22.380
Saliva works because of the simple
fact that larger saliva volumes

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:25.800
allow increased amounts of volatile
sulfur compounds to enter solution.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:29.340
Instead of off-gassing,
the gases dissolve in the fluid.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:32.460
This can also be accomplished
by just drinking some water

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:35.880
or by using chewing gum since
that causes your mouth to water.

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:40.412
Is there any flavor we can add
that makes it work better?

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:43.920
A study on eucalyptus-flavored
chewing gum was published.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:47.113
People were randomized to chew
a high-concentration eucalyptus gum

00:01:47.113 --> 00:01:49.740
5 times a day,
a low-concentration gum,

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:51.000
or just a flavorless gum.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:55.200
And both of the eucalyptus-flavored
gums significantly beat out

00:01:55.200 --> 00:02:02.000
the control group gum, both
quantitatively and koala-tatively.

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:05.460
OK, but where are you going
to find eucalyptus gum?

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:07.420
What about the effect
of cinnamon?

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:10.229
Against one bad
breath-associated bacteria,

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:12.348
cinnamon oil kicked tush,

00:02:12.348 --> 00:02:15.050
suggesting it’s a promising
substance to incorporate

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:17.700
into oral hygiene products
for controlling bad breath,

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:20.040
but it utterly failed to control

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:22.200
four other types
of bad breath bacteria.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:25.080
These studies were all just
done in petri dishes, though.

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:27.240
You don’t really know
if cinnamon-flavored gum

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:32.280
would help with bad breath
until...you put it to the test.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:34.260
The short-term
germ-killing effect

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:36.360
of sugar-sweetened
cinnamon chewing gum

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:39.840
on bacteria associated
with bad breath and…

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:42.120
the researchers conclude
that commercially available

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:43.920
sugar-sweetened
cinnamon chewing gum

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:45.900
may indeed benefit bad breath,

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:48.600
but if you look at the
actual data,

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:52.560
the same gum without
cinnamon oil did no different

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:54.540
than the gum
with cinnamon oil.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:57.480
Why were they so eager
to spin the results

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:59.640
to suggest some sort
of unique benefit?

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:04.421
Perhaps because the study
was funded by Wrigley.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:08.280
Bottomline, gum chewing per se
is considered to be helpful

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:10.980
in reducing bad breath
by stimulating saliva flow

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:13.069
and mechanical cleansing,

00:03:13.069 --> 00:03:17.168
even if there’s no “active”
ingredients in it.

